      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. I'll look into implementing a standardized \"Unknown\" string in the creator field to maintain machine-readability. \n\nMoving slightly away from the creator field, I'm also seeing some peculiar issues with the physical condition and provenance of several map sets. Some of them seem to have had elements \"missing\" or altered in ways that don't look like standard wear and tear—it’s almost as if someone went in and subtly meddled with the collection, or as if some of the more \"unidentifiable\" details in the maps themselves are meant to be purposefully elusive or disruptive to the viewer. \n\nWhen we encounter items where the physical history or the content itself feels... deliberately tricky or structurally \"off\" in a way that defies standard cataloging, how do archives typically categorize that kind of anomaly? Is there a way to record metadata for \"irregular\" or \"anomalous\" items that captures that sense of a disrupted or obscured history without sounding unprofessional?",
